{
 "lead": "A sensor does not jump to a new reading the instant the input changes: heat must flow, a mass must move, a diaphragm must flex. The delay is captured by a mathematical model: zero order (instant), first order (one energy store, one time constant τ) or second order (two energy stores, natural frequency ω<sub>n</sub> and damping ζ). These constants decide whether a thermocouple can follow a furnace or a catheter can follow a heartbeat.",
 "prereq": "You need: Chapter 1.4 for sensitivity K and full scale (<a href=\"#static-characteristics-accuracy-precision-resolution-sensitivity\">static characteristics</a>), and first-order differential equations and complex numbers from mathematics.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Transfer function and zero order",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Write the input as x(t) and output as y(t). In the Laplace domain, the transfer function is H(s) = Y(s)/X(s). Here s is the complex frequency variable, Y(s) is the Laplace transform of the output, and X(s) is that of the input. H(s) is the sensor's mathematical model: it tells how the sensor modifies any input. Some syllabi call it the mathematical model of the transducer. The order is the number of independent energy stores."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A zero-order sensor has H(s) = K, so y(t) = K x(t) at every instant. K is the sensitivity, in output units per input unit (from static characteristics). The output has no lag. A pure resistor divider measuring a slowly varying voltage is close to zero order. A potentiometer is close to zero order when its load resistance is much larger than its own resistance. A heavy load causes a loading error: the output becomes a nonlinear function of wiper position, but the response stays instant. The real lag in a potentiometer comes from the mass of the wiper, which makes it second order at high speeds. Zero order is the ideal we compare against."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "First order: one time constant",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A first-order sensor obeys τ(dy/dt) + y = Kx. τ is the time constant in seconds, dy/dt is the rate of change of output, and K is the static sensitivity. Its transfer function is H(s) = K/(1 + τs)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a step of size A at t = 0, starting from zero, y(t) = K A (1 − e<sup>−t/τ</sup>). At t = τ the output is 63.2 % of final; at 3τ it is 95.0 %; at 5τ it is 99.3 %. So τ is not the settling time."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a ramp input of slope R, the output becomes a ramp of the same slope after a start-up, but lags it by τR. A thermocouple dipped into a bath is the standard picture. Its bead has mass m, specific heat c, surface area A<sub>s</sub> and convection coefficient h, so τ = mc/(hA<sub>s</sub>). Here m is in kg, c in J/(kg·K), A<sub>s</sub> in m² and h in W/(m²·K), so τ comes out in seconds. A larger bead or weaker convection gives a larger τ."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a sinusoidal input x = A sin(ωt), the amplitude ratio is 1/√(1 + (ωτ)²) and the phase lag is tan⁻¹(ωτ). At ω = 1/τ the ratio is 0.707, the −3 dB bandwidth. The impulse response is (K/τ)e<sup>−t/τ</sup>."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Second order: natural frequency and damping",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A second-order sensor obeys (1/ω<sub>n</sub>²)(d²y/dt²) + (2ζ/ω<sub>n</sub>)(dy/dt) + y = Kx. ω<sub>n</sub> is the undamped natural frequency in rad/s; ζ is the damping ratio, dimensionless. The transfer function is H(s) = K / (s²/ω<sub>n</sub>² + 2ζs/ω<sub>n</sub> + 1). A mass m on a spring k with damper c gives ω<sub>n</sub> = √(k/m) and ζ = c/(2√(km))."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a step of size A: when ζ < 1 the sensor is underdamped, the output rises past the final value and rings at the damped natural frequency ω<sub>d</sub> = ω<sub>n</sub>√(1 − ζ²) in rad/s. The first peak is at the peak time t<sub>p</sub> = π/ω<sub>d</sub>, and the peak overshoot is M<sub>p</sub> = exp(−πζ/√(1 − ζ²)), a fraction of the final value. As a fraction, M<sub>p</sub> = 0.527 means 52.7 %. At ζ = 1 the system is critically damped: the fastest response with no overshoot. At ζ > 1 it is overdamped: slow, with no overshoot. Most sensors aim for ζ around 0.6 to 0.8."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a sinusoid, put r = ω/ω<sub>n</sub>. The amplitude ratio is 1/√((1 − r²)² + (2ζr)²). Resonance peaks near r = √(1 − 2ζ²), and only for small ζ is the peak sharp; it exists only when ζ < 0.707. A ramp input leaves a steady lag of 2ζ/ω<sub>n</sub> times the slope. The impulse response is a sine at the damped natural frequency ω<sub>d</sub> = ω<sub>n</sub>√(1 − ζ²) rad/s, whose amplitude decays as e<sup>−ζω<sub>n</sub> t</sup>."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The four standard test inputs, side by side (unit sensitivity, starting from rest):"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Input",
      "Zero order",
      "First order",
      "Second order (ζ < 1)"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Step A",
       "A at once",
       "A(1 − e<sup>−t/τ</sup>)",
       "overshoot M<sub>p</sub>, rings at ω<sub>d</sub>, settles in about 4/(ζω<sub>n</sub>)"
      ],
      [
       "Ramp Rt",
       "Rt, no lag",
       "lags by τR",
       "lags by 2ζR/ω<sub>n</sub>"
      ],
      [
       "Impulse",
       "a spike",
       "(1/τ)e<sup>−t/τ</sup>",
       "decaying sine at ω<sub>d</sub>"
      ],
      [
       "Sine",
       "no change",
       "ratio 1/√(1 + (ωτ)²), lag tan⁻¹(ωτ)",
       "ratio 1/√((1 − r²)² + (2ζr)²)"
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
