   "aria": "Three plots. Top: thermocouple reading against time from 0 to 10 seconds rising from 25 towards 100 degrees, with 72.4 at 2 seconds and 96.3 at 6 seconds marked. Bottom left: second order step response with damping 0.2, peaking at 1.527 at 0.51 seconds and settling by about 3.2 seconds. Bottom right: amplitude ratio against frequency up to 40 hertz for a 25 hertz catheter system; the damping 0.2 curve peaks near 24 hertz and is 2.08 at 20 hertz, the damping 0.6 curve stays near 1 and is 0.975 at 20 hertz."
  }
 },
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A thermocouple bead has τ = 2 s. It is taken from 25 °C and dipped into a bath at 100 °C. (a) Find the reading at t = 2 s and t = 6 s. (b) If the bath is ramped at 2 °C/s, find the steady lag. (c) A pressure diaphragm has f<sub>n</sub> = 1 Hz and ζ = 0.2. Find overshoot, peak time and 2 % settling time.",
   "steps": [
    "Step size A = 100 − 25 = 75 °C.",
    "At t = 2 s = τ: T = 25 + 75(1 − e<sup>−1</sup>) = 25 + 75 × 0.6321 = 72.4 °C.",
    "At t = 6 s = 3τ: T = 25 + 75(1 − e<sup>−3</sup>) = 25 + 75 × 0.9502 = 96.3 °C.",
    "Ramp lag = τ × slope = 2 s × 2 °C/s = 4 °C. The reading stays 4 °C below the bath.",
    "ω<sub>n</sub> = 2πf<sub>n</sub> = 2π × 1 = 6.283 rad/s.",
    "ω<sub>d</sub> = ω<sub>n</sub>√(1 − ζ²) = 6.283√0.96 = 6.156 rad/s.",
    "Overshoot M<sub>p</sub> = exp(−π × 0.2/√0.96) = exp(−0.641) = 0.527, i.e. 52.7 %.",
    "Peak time t<sub>p</sub> = π/ω<sub>d</sub> = π/6.156 = 0.51 s.",
    "2 % settling time ≈ 4/(ζω<sub>n</sub>) = 4/(0.2 × 6.283) = 4/1.257 = 3.2 s."
   ],
   "answer": "The thermocouple reads 72.4 °C at 2 s and 96.3 °C at 6 s, lags a 2 °C/s ramp by 4 °C; the diaphragm overshoots by 52.7 %, peaks at 0.51 s and settles in about 3.2 s.",
   "output": ""
  },
  {
   "question": "A fluid-filled catheter and its pressure transducer measure arterial pressure. The system is second order with f<sub>n</sub> = 25 Hz and ζ = 0.2. The heart beats at 120 per minute (2 Hz), and the waveform carries useful harmonics up to the 10th. Find the amplitude ratio and phase lag at 2 Hz and at 20 Hz, the resonant frequency and peak ratio, and the ratio at 20 Hz if the damping is raised to ζ = 0.6.",
   "steps": [
    "Frequency ratios: r = f/f<sub>n</sub>. At 2 Hz, r = 2/25 = 0.08. At 20 Hz, r = 20/25 = 0.8.",
    "At r = 0.08: (1 − r²)² = 0.9936² = 0.98724 and (2ζr)² = 0.032² = 0.00102. Ratio = 1/√0.98827 = 1.006, phase lag = tan⁻¹(0.032/0.9936) = 1.8°. The fundamental passes almost unchanged.",
    "At r = 0.8: (1 − 0.64)² = 0.1296 and (2 × 0.2 × 0.8)² = 0.1024. Ratio = 1/√0.2320 = 2.076, phase lag = tan⁻¹(0.32/0.36) = 41.6°. The 10th harmonic is more than doubled.",
    "Resonance: r<sub>r</sub> = √(1 − 2ζ²) = √0.92 = 0.959, so f<sub>r</sub> = 0.959 × 25 = 24.0 Hz. Peak ratio = 1/(2ζ√(1 − ζ²)) = 1/(0.4 × 0.9798) = 2.55.",
    "With ζ = 0.6 at r = 0.8: (2 × 0.6 × 0.8)² = 0.9216, sum 1.0512, ratio = 1/√1.0512 = 0.975 (−2.5 %). The resonant peak moves to r = √0.28 = 0.529 and is only 1/(1.2 × 0.8) = 1.04.",
    "Meaning: at ζ = 0.2 the monitor shows a falsely high systolic peak (the ringing harmonics add on top); at ζ = 0.6 every harmonic up to 20 Hz stays within about 4 %."
   ],
   "answer": "At 2 Hz: ratio 1.006, lag 1.8°. At 20 Hz: ratio 2.08, lag 41.6°. Resonance at 24.0 Hz with peak ratio 2.55. With ζ = 0.6 the 20 Hz ratio is 0.975, and the whole band stays within about 4 %."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Reading τ as the time to reach the final value — it is the time to reach 63.2 %.",
  "Using the overshoot formula for ζ = 1 or ζ > 1; it only holds for ζ < 1.",
  "Putting f<sub>n</sub> in Hz directly into formulas that need ω<sub>n</sub> in rad/s; convert with ω<sub>n</sub> = 2πf<sub>n</sub>.",
  "Treating a catheter–transducer system as zero order. Air bubbles and soft tubing lower f<sub>n</sub>; with low damping the systolic peak reads high."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "What order is a sensor with H(s) = K/(1 + τs), and what does τ represent?",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "First order; τ is the time constant in seconds."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "What percentage of a first-order step is reached at t = τ and t = 3τ?",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "63.2 % and 95.0 %."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does a larger thermocouple bead have a larger time constant?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "More thermal mass (larger mc) and often less surface per volume; τ = mc/(hA<sub>s</sub>) rises."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A first-order sensor with τ = 0.5 s sees a 10 °C step. Find the output change at t = 1 s.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "10(1 − e<sup>−2</sup>) = 10 × 0.8647 = 8.65 °C."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A second-order sensor has f<sub>n</sub> = 2 Hz and ζ = 0.5. Find ω<sub>n</sub>, ω<sub>d</sub> and the overshoot.",
   "marks": 5,
   "a": "ω<sub>n</sub> = 12.57 rad/s; ω<sub>d</sub> = 12.57√0.75 = 10.88 rad/s; M<sub>p</sub> = exp(−π × 0.5/√0.75) = 0.163, i.e. 16.3 %."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Compare ζ = 0.05 and ζ = 0.7 for a second-order sensor under a step and a sine near ω<sub>n</sub>.",
   "marks": 6,
   "a": "ζ = 0.05 rings strongly with about 85 % overshoot and a sharp resonance; ζ = 0.7 gives about 4.6 % overshoot and a flat response, so it is more usable but slower."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. In intensive care, a fast-flush test on the pressure line shows the ringing and gives f<sub>n</sub> and ζ at the bedside. Search next for \"fast flush test\" and \"sensor frequency response\"."
